# gow-native-pc (patch 72): the per-batch GL prelude only issues the calls whose arguments changed.
#
# Problem: every GS batch re-issued Viewport / Enable(SCISSOR_TEST) / Scissor / Disable(SCISSOR_TEST) /
# Disable(CULL_FACE) even when none of their arguments had changed since the previous batch. [gs-cost2] put that
# prelude at pre_fbo=141-194 ms per 5 s at 0.5x with 12.4k batches (11 us of the ~90 us a batch costs), the third
# largest item of the GS worker behind texture decoding and the draw call itself.
#
# Change: three helpers (setViewport / setScissorRect / setScissorTest) remember what is bound and skip the driver
# call when nothing changed; every writer in the renderer goes through them. Two calls are removed outright:
#   * Disable(CULL_FACE) - nothing in the backend ever calls Enable(CULL_FACE), so it was a no-op issued 12k times/5 s;
#   * the Disable(SCISSOR_TEST) at the end of draw() - the next batch turns it back on, and every non-draw path
#     that needs it off (CLUT readback, shuffle, depth clear, ClearFramebuffer, Present) calls setScissorTest(false).
# GOW_GS_STATE_CACHE=0 restores the unconditional calls (it does not restore the two removed ones: measure those by
# reverting the patch).
#
# Measured (i3-4170 + HD 4400, Asia disc SLPM_670.10, 0.5x, PS2X_GS_WORKER=1, VU1 x64 fast, GOW_GS_COST=1, 5 s
# windows, 22-25 s runs, the two settings alternating inside the same build):
#   pre_fbo          141-194 ms/5 s  ->  47-94 ms/5 s      (the item this patch targets)
#   one_ms/batch     83.5-92.8 us    ->  69.4-71.8 us      (-14..-23 %; batches got heavier in the same runs, so the
#                                                           per-batch figure is the comparable one, not guest fps)
#   GL errors        0 in every run (GOW_GS_HW_ERRTRACE and the per-batch GetError budget)
# The scene (opening level, game-driven) differs too much between runs to quote a guest-fps delta honestly.
#
# Also in this patch, no effect measured on this disc, kept because they are correct and cost nothing when unused:
#   * decodeDirect() gives each colour format its own TEXA row loop instead of one applyTexa() call per texel
#     ([gs-hw] direct=0/0K on this disc: it decodes no direct-colour image, so this path is not exercised here);
#   * [gs-hw] now reports direct=/idx= decode counts and texels. That is what found the real texture cost: ~800 index
#     decodes per 5 s, 80 M texels, i.e. the paletted path (indexTextureFor -> GSMem::ReadIndexRun), not this one.
#     A fast path for PSMT8 rows was written and dropped again: the per-pixel checker GOW_GS_IDXCHECK showed the
#     first two pixels of row 0 of bp=35a0 at bytes 36 and 178, so P8 rows are not linear and a run cannot be a
#     memcpy. That measurement is why this patch does not touch ReadIndexRun.
#   * [gs-hw] also reports blk=<written blocks>/<image blocks> and box=<their bounding rectangle>/<image area>, taken
#     when an image is re-decoded after an upload invalidated it. Measured on the Asia disc: blk=41-42 %, box=91-93 %.
#     That is the answer to "why not re-decode only the part that changed": the game rewrites scattered atlas entries,
#     so the rectangle that covers them is almost the whole image. A partial re-decode would save nothing and add one
#     GL call per rectangle. Both counters are kept so the next person does not have to measure this again.
#
# What was tried and is not in here:
#   * PSMT8 rows as a memcpy run (see above) - measured wrong, removed;
#   * hoisting the page-table lookup out of the texel loop (precomputed row of offsets, page base once per 128
#     texels) - measured 3.2 ns/texel against 3.1 ns/texel for the original, i.e. the compiler already hoists it;
#     removed rather than kept as dead complexity;
#   * partial texture re-decode driven by the written block range - killed by the box=91-93 % measurement.
#
# Verification: the diff reproduces the working tree exactly - `patch -p1 < 72-gs-batch-prelude.patch` applied to
# the pre-change tree yields a file byte-identical to the one in engine/runtime (checked), and the runtime built from
# it ran the Asia disc with 0 GL errors and the title/legal screens rendering correctly (host_shot.png).
#
# Layout note: patches 59..71 are still uncommitted in this tree and do not reapply to 471c831 on their own (patch 59
# fails on gs_gl.cpp), so the chain cannot be replayed from scratch to prove this file's base. It is a diff against
# the tree that carries 59..71.

